# Kiro-style specs

Specs live at `.kiro/specs/<id>/` as four files. Read **in order**: `README.md` →
`requirements.md` → `design.md` → `tasks.md`. Do not implement from memory of an
older spec.

## `tasks.md` is a living checklist

It is not a read-only brief. After each task whose **Definition of done** is
literally true:

1. Flip `- [ ]` to `- [x]` in that file in the same sitting (same commit if you
   are already committing).
2. Leave **human-triggered / live-spend / budget-capped** tasks unchecked.
3. If you batched work, still update every completed box before you stop. An
   all-empty checklist after a landed implementation is a process bug.

Do **not** check a box because the code exists. Check it because each DoD
bullet is true (tests, fixtures, journal note, flag default, etc.).

## How to execute

- Prefer **one task at a time**, in order. The spec's Cursor prompt shape is the
  default: implement N.M, run the listed quality commands, stop.
- When the user says "implement this spec", do every in-repo task; skip only
  tasks marked human-triggered spend unless they explicitly approve spend.
- New behavior on a normal run ships **off by default** until the spec says it
  earned its place.
- Record suite/CI failures in a journal entry (what failed, why, the fix). Do
  not bury that trail only in chat.

## Do not

- Invent a fifth spec document or rewrite requirements/design unless asked.
- Treat "I built it" as "the checklist can stay empty".
- Enable a billed eval, live arm, or `--execute` sweep from an agent session.
